### What is a Trie and how does it support this problem?

A Trie, also called a prefix tree, is a tree data structure where each node represents a character in a string and holds child links for the next characters. Inserting a word walks from the root, creating child nodes as needed, and marks the final node as an end-of-word. This structure allows prefix-based lookups in time proportional to the word length.

For the National Bank of India, Rajesh builds a Trie of transaction reference codes so support agents can search for references that match a partial pattern.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class WordDictionary {
    private static class TrieNode {
        Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
        boolean isEndOfWord = false;
    }

    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    public void addWord(String word) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.computeIfAbsent(c, k -> new TrieNode());
        }
        node.isEndOfWord = true;
    }
}
```

### How does the wildcard search work?

Search is a recursive depth-first traversal parameterised by the current index in the pattern and the current Trie node. When the pattern character is a normal letter, the search follows exactly one child link matching that letter. When the character is a dot, the search fans out to every child of the current node and succeeds if any branch succeeds. The base case is when the index reaches the end of the pattern, at which point the search returns whether the current node is marked as an end-of-word.

```java
public boolean search(String word) {
    return dfs(word, 0, root);
}

private boolean dfs(String word, int index, TrieNode node) {
    if (index == word.length()) {
        return node.isEndOfWord;  // must be a complete word, not just a prefix
    }
    char c = word.charAt(index);
    if (c == '.') {
        // Dot: try every child
        for (TrieNode child : node.children.values()) {
            if (dfs(word, index + 1, child)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    } else {
        // Normal character: follow one exact child
        TrieNode child = node.children.get(c);
        return child != null && dfs(word, index + 1, child);
    }
}
```

### Why does the dot fan out to all children instead of iterating characters?

The dot must try every possible character at that position, but we do not know which characters exist. Instead of iterating over all 26 lowercase letters and checking each one, we iterate over the children map, which contains only the characters that actually have entries. This is more efficient because a node typically has far fewer than 26 children.

This matters in the banking domain because the reference code alphabet might include digits and letters, making a fixed iteration over all characters wasteful.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DotFanOutDemo {
    // Iterating node.children.values() is better than:
    // for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
    //     TrieNode child = node.children.get(ch);
    //     if (child != null && dfs(...)) return true;
    // }
    // Because children.values() only visits existing children.

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordDictionary dict = new WordDictionary();
        dict.addWord("TXN001");
        dict.addWord("TXN002");
        dict.addWord("REF001");

        System.out.println(dict.search("TXN001"));  // true: exact match
        System.out.println(dict.search("TXN.01"));  // true: dot matches 0
        System.out.println(dict.search("...001"));  // true: dots match T, X, N
        System.out.println(dict.search("ABC001"));  // false: no such prefix
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
true
false
```

### How is this different from a regular Trie search?

| Feature | Regular Trie search | Wildcard Trie search |
|---|---|---|
| Character handling | follow one exact child | follow one child for letter, all children for dot |
| Time for length L | O(L) | O(L) best case, O(N * L) worst case with all dots |
| Recursion | iterative or recursive | must be recursive (DFS for branching) |
| Use case | autocomplete, prefix check | pattern matching with unknown characters |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TrieComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordDictionary dict = new WordDictionary();
        dict.addWord("loan");
        dict.addWord("lore");
        dict.addWord("loft");

        // Regular search: follows one path, O(L)
        System.out.println(dict.search("loan")); // true

        // Wildcard search: dot at index 2 fans out to a, r, f
        System.out.println(dict.search("lo.n")); // true (matches loan)
        System.out.println(dict.search("lo.e")); // true (matches lore)
        System.out.println(dict.search("lo.x")); // false (no match)
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
true
false
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

The addWord method receives only lowercase English letters. The search method receives lowercase English letters and the dot character. Both words and patterns have length between 1 and 25. The data structure can hold up to 10,000 words. The end-of-word check at the base case is critical: without it, a prefix that is not a complete word would falsely match.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class EndOfWordImportance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordDictionary dict = new WordDictionary();
        dict.addWord("payment");

        // "pay" is a prefix of "payment" but not a stored word
        System.out.println(dict.search("pay"));     // false: not end-of-word
        System.out.println(dict.search("payment")); // true: end-of-word
        System.out.println(dict.search("pa.ment")); // true: dot matches y
    }
}
```

```text
false
true
true
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a search pattern that is entirely dots, which must fan out at every level and could explore a large part of the Trie. Another edge case is searching for a word that was never added, where the search should fail gracefully at the first missing child link. A third edge case is adding the same word twice, which should not change behaviour because the end-of-word flag is already set.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TrieEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordDictionary dict = new WordDictionary();
        dict.addWord("emi");
        dict.addWord("emi");  // duplicate add: no change

        System.out.println(dict.search("..."));    // true: all dots match e, m, i
        System.out.println(dict.search("...."));   // false: pattern too long
        System.out.println(dict.search("xyz"));    // false: no such word
        System.out.println(dict.search(".m."));    // true: matches emi
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
false
true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the end-of-word check at the base case, which causes the search to match prefixes that are not complete words. Another mistake is not handling the dot case by iterating the children map and instead trying to iterate all 26 letters, which is wasteful. A third mistake is returning false too early in the dot case without trying all children.

Wrong code that skips end-of-word check:

```java
// WRONG: returns true even for prefixes
private boolean dfsBad(String word, int index, TrieNode node) {
    if (index == word.length()) {
        return true; // BUG: should check node.isEndOfWord
    }
    // ... rest of search
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: only matches complete words
private boolean dfsGood(String word, int index, TrieNode node) {
    if (index == word.length()) {
        return node.isEndOfWord; // must be a word ending
    }
    // ... rest of search
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that the worst-case search time with all dots is O(N * L) where N is the total number of nodes and L is the pattern length, because every dot branches into all children. In production, they would limit the number of dots allowed or add a timeout. They also know that using a `char[26]` array instead of a HashMap for children trades memory for faster lookups, and that this data structure is the foundation of regex engines, spell checkers, and autocomplete systems.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TrieArrayOptimisation {
    // Array-based TrieNode: faster lookups, fixed 26-char alphabet
    static class FastTrieNode {
        FastTrieNode[] children = new FastTrieNode[26];
        boolean isEndOfWord = false;
    }

    static FastTrieNode root = new FastTrieNode();

    static void addWord(String word) {
        FastTrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            int idx = c - 'a';
            if (node.children[idx] == null) {
                node.children[idx] = new FastTrieNode();
            }
            node = node.children[idx];
        }
        node.isEndOfWord = true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        addWord("transfer");
        addWord("transaction");
        System.out.println("Added words to array-based Trie");
    }
}
```

```text
Added words to array-based Trie
